<div class="article-shell">

  <!-- ARTICLE HEADER -->
  <header class="article-header reveal">
    <h1 class="article-title">Testing Data as a <em>Future Simulation</em></h1>
    <p class="article-lead">A testing set answers a narrower question than its name may suggest: how did the model perform on held-out cases from the available historical data? That result can inform future use only when the testing cases, measurement process, and operating conditions are relevant to the decision ahead.</p>
  </header>

  <main id="main">

  <div class="article-body">

    <div class="reveal">
      <p>Holding data back provides evidence about whether a model can support decisions involving cases it did not use to learn.</p>

      <p>For example, if the model will be used to prioritize churn interventions, the business needs to know:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>Does the model correctly identify high-risk subscribers?</li>
        <li>Does it miss many subscribers who actually churn?</li>
        <li>Does it incorrectly flag too many subscribers who would have stayed?</li>
        <li>Does it perform consistently across different customer groups?</li>
      </ul>

    <p>None of these can be answered reliably by evaluating the model on the same data it used to learn. Each question requires held-out outcomes and a comparison between the model's predictions and what occurred.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="pull-quote reveal">
      <p>Is the test data representative of the future population where this model will be used?</p>
    </div>

    <div class="reveal">
      <p>If the test data is not representative, the model's performance may look better or worse than it will be in reality.</p>

      <p>For example, suppose the testing set overrepresents long-tenured subscribers and underrepresents new promotional subscribers. The model may appear to perform well overall but fail on the exact group the business most needs to understand.</p>

    </div>

    <div class="reveal">
      <h2>Checking Whether the Testing Set Represents the Future</h2>

      <p>Start with a comparison you can ask for in a meeting. Have someone describe who is in the testing set: how many are long-tenured subscribers, how many joined on a promotion, and what mix of plans and regions is represented. Compare those proportions with the subscribers the model will be applied to. Material differences limit how directly the test result applies to the deployment population.</p>

      <p>Representation counts do not show how the model performs within each group. A model can be right more than 80 percent of the time across the whole testing set while being barely better than a coin flip on new promotional subscribers, because that group is too small to change the total much. Ask for performance by relevant segment, not only the overall figure.</p>

      <div class="takeaway" role="note" aria-labelledby="takeaway-accuracy-label">
        <div class="takeaway-label" id="takeaway-accuracy-label">Takeaway</div>
        <p class="takeaway-text">An overall accuracy figure can hide the group the business most needs to understand. Ask for results by segment before treating a headline number as evidence.</p>
      </div>

      <p>Two questions accompany any segment result. First, ask what the number is being compared against. Suppose 12 percent of a segment churns. A rule that ignores the data entirely and says nobody will churn is right 88 percent of the time, and no model is needed to apply it. That 88 percent is the bar any model has to clear, so a reported <span class="vocab" title="Accuracy is the share of cases the model classifies correctly. Here, it is the number of correct calls divided by the number of subscribers in the segment.">accuracy</span> of 90 percent clears it by only 2 percentage points. Second, ask how many records support the result. A rate based on 180 cases is generally less precise than the same rate based on 1,300, so ask for the count and an uncertainty interval alongside the rate, especially for underrepresented segments.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="reveal">
      <h2>The Part a Testing Set Cannot Rehearse</h2>

      <p>The rehearsal has a limit. A testing set is normally taken from the same historical file as the training set, which means it covers the same stretch of time. Holding it back shows the model can handle subscribers it has not seen. It does not show the model can handle a period it has not seen.</p>

      <p>Anything that changes between the period the data covers and the period the model runs in is missing from both halves of a random split. That includes a price increase, a new content slate, or a competitor launching in the same market. Strong test performance supports use on similar held-out cases; it does not establish that later conditions will behave the same way. Out-of-time evaluation can test a later historical period, and monitoring after launch is needed to detect changes that appear in operation.</p>

      <div class="takeaway" role="note" aria-labelledby="takeaway-rehearse-label">
        <div class="takeaway-label" id="takeaway-rehearse-label">Takeaway</div>
        <p class="takeaway-text">Held-out testing checks unseen records, not unseen future conditions.</p>
      </div>

      <p>Those are the checks. These are the questions to ask around them. They cover where the data came from, and who is answerable for what the model does:</p>

      <ul>
        <li>Who selected the training and testing data?</li>
        <li>Which segments were checked separately, and which were never looked at?</li>
        <li>What are the consequences of false positives and false negatives?</li>
        <li>Who is accountable if the model output causes harm or poor decisions?</li>
        <li>What process exists for monitoring the model after deployment?</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Each question requires input from business, operational, risk, or governance roles as well as the data science team.</p>

      <p>If there is a problem with the model output, accountability cannot sit only with the algorithm. The organization needs clear ownership for how the model is built, tested, approved, used, and monitored.</p>

      <p>Use these questions in this week&rsquo;s exploratory Colab activity. You will review a churn model built by someone else, with results that already look strong, and decide whether the evidence supports business use.</p>
    </div>

